**Sec. 20001**
This section appropriates approximately $7.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to improve military quality of life, including $2.9 billion for housing allowances, $2 billion for the Defense Health Program, and $1 billion for unaccompanied housing modernization. It also allocates funds for specific initiatives such as $230.48 million for Marine Corps barracks, $590 million to extend Temporary Lodging Expense Allowance to 21 days, and $100 million each for tuition assistance, child care fee assistance, and Impact Aid payments. Additionally, the bill temporarily increases the authorized investment percentage in privatized military housing projects from 33⅓% to 60% and expands authority for the Secretary concerned to contract with private entities for unaccompanied housing through September 30, 2029.

**Sec. 20002**
This section appropriates approximately $23.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to enhance shipbuilding capabilities, including $5.4 billion for two additional Guided Missile Destroyers and $4.6 billion for a second Virginia-class submarine. Significant funds are directed toward industrial base expansion, such as $750 million for supplier development, $500 million for dry-dock capability, and $500 million for advanced manufacturing techniques. The bill also allocates $2.725 billion for T-AO oilers, $1.8 billion for Landing Ship Medium procurement, and substantial amounts for unmanned systems, including $2.1 billion for medium unmanned surface vessels and $1.3 billion for unmanned underwater vehicles.

**Sec. 20003**
This section appropriates approximately $22.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to strengthen integrated air and missile defense, with $7.2 billion for military space-based sensors and $5.6 billion for space-based and boost phase intercept capabilities. It includes $2.55 billion for general missile defense capabilities, $2.2 billion for hypersonic defense systems, and $2 billion for air moving target indicator satellites. Further allocations include $1.975 billion for ground-based missile defense radars, $800 million for next-generation intercontinental ballistic missile defense, and $500 million for national security space launch infrastructure.

**Sec. 20004**
This section appropriates approximately $14.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to bolster munitions and supply chain resiliency, including $2 billion for critical minerals stockpiles and $1 billion each for automated munitions factories and one-way attack unmanned aerial systems. Specific munitions funding includes $688 million for long-range cruise missiles, $630 million for long-range Navy air defense missiles, and $400 million for heavyweight torpedoes. The bill also provides $5 billion for critical minerals supply chains and $3.3 billion for Industrial Base Fund grants, alongside $500 million for counter-unmanned aerial systems and $1 billion for solid rocket motor industrial base investments.

**Sec. 20005**
This section appropriates approximately $10.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to scale low-cost weapons and innovative technologies, including $2 billion for Defense Innovation Unit commercial technology scaling and $1.5 billion for low-cost cruise missiles. It allocates $1.4 billion for small unmanned aerial system industrial base expansion, $1 billion for accelerating innovative technology procurement, and $1 billion for defense industrial base competition enhancements. Additional funds support specific technologies such as $750 million for military logistics, $650 million for joint prototyping, $600 million for Strategic Capabilities Office programs, and $500 million for 5G/6G military technologies.

**Sec. 20006**
This section appropriates $380 million to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to improve Department of Defense efficiency and cybersecurity. The funds include $200 million for deploying automation and artificial intelligence to accelerate financial statement audits, $150 million for business systems replacement to support audits, and $20 million for Defense Advanced Research Projects Agency cybersecurity programs. An additional $10 million is allocated to improve the budgetary and programmatic infrastructure of the Office of the Secretary of Defense.

**Sec. 20007**
This section appropriates approximately $7.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to enhance air superiority, including $3.15 billion to increase F-15EX aircraft production and $750 million to accelerate the FA/XX aircraft program. It allocates $678 million for the Collaborative Combat Aircraft program, $600 million for Air Force long-range strike aircraft, and $500 million for Navy long-range strike aircraft. Further funding includes $474 million for EA-37B production, $440 million for C-130J production, $400 million for F-47 aircraft production, and $361.22 million to prevent the retirement of F-22 aircraft.

**Sec. 20008**
This section appropriates approximately $13.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense and $3.9 billion to the National Nuclear Security Administration (NNSA) for fiscal year 2025 to enhance nuclear forces. Defense funds include $4.5 billion for B-21 bomber production expansion, $2.5 billion for Sentinel intercontinental ballistic missile risk reduction, and $2 billion for nuclear-armed sea-launched cruise missiles. NNSA funds include $1 billion for facility construction, $750 million each for primary and secondary capability modernization, and $540 million for deferred maintenance, while also supporting uranium enrichment and spent fuel reprocessing evaluations.

**Sec. 20009**
This section appropriates approximately $10.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to improve United States Indo-Pacific Command capabilities, including $3.65 billion for military satellites and $1 billion for offensive cyber operations. It allocates $1.1 billion for infrastructure development, $850 million for military article replenishment, and $500 million for economic competition effects and personnel costs. Specific regional and technological investments include $365 million for Army exercises, $200 million for the Guam Defense System, $150 million for ground moving target indicator satellites, and $1 billion for the X-37B spacecraft program.

**Sec. 20010**
This section appropriates approximately $15.5 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to improve readiness, including $2.5 billion for Air Force facilities sustainment and $2.118 billion for Air Force aircraft spares. It allocates $2 billion for Navy depot and shipyard modernization, $1.5 billion for Army depot modernization, and $1.5 billion for increased depot and shipyard maintenance activities. Further funds support specific units and equipment, such as $1.64 billion for Special Operations Command, $700 million for amphibious ship spares, $500 million for National Guard readiness, and $400 million for Marine Corps capabilities.

**Sec. 20011**
This section appropriates $1 billion to the Secretary of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to support border operations and counter-drug missions. The funds are designated for deploying military personnel, operations and maintenance for border support, counter-narcotics and counter-transnational criminal organization missions, and the temporary detention of migrants on Department of Defense installations. These activities are to be conducted in accordance with chapter 15 of title 10, United States Code.

**Sec. 20012**
This section appropriates $10 million to the Inspector General of the Department of Defense for fiscal year 2025 to oversee activities funded in this title. The oversight focuses on monitoring programs with mutual technological dependencies, related data management and ownership considerations, and those particularly vulnerable to supply chain disruptions and long lead time components.

**Sec. 20013**
This section authorizes the appropriation of funds for military construction, land acquisition, and military family housing functions for each military department as specified in the title. It requires the Secretary of each military department to submit a detailed spending plan by project to the Senate and House Committees on Armed Services within 30 days of enactment. [end of text]


